WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.280
Whether here on Earth, in orbit, or on the path to deep space, NASA is pushing the boundaries

00:05.280 --> 00:07.720
of exploration and discovery.

00:07.720 --> 00:10.600
Here's what's new this week in Your NASA Minute.

00:10.600 --> 00:16.080
On Thursday, NASA released the Independent Investigative Report on the 2024 Starliner

00:16.080 --> 00:18.600
Crew Flight Test to the public.

00:18.600 --> 00:21.600
Transparency is not a weakness, it is a strength.

00:21.600 --> 00:24.760
American trusts NASA with the hardest endeavors ever attempted.

00:24.760 --> 00:29.400
The world looks to us for the discoveries, achievements, and leadership only this agency

00:29.400 --> 00:32.920
can deliver, and that requires putting the mission and the crew first.

00:32.920 --> 00:37.000
NASA is taking corrective actions to address the findings of the report, and will enact

00:37.000 --> 00:41.480
meaningful change throughout the agency to improve safety and achieve success on our

00:41.480 --> 00:42.720
missions.

00:42.720 --> 00:47.160
Following a successful wet dress rehearsal, NASA is working toward a potential March launch

00:47.160 --> 00:52.320
opportunity for Artemis II, with teams targeting no earlier than March 6th.

00:52.320 --> 00:56.200
During the test, engineers fueled the Space Launch System rocket and demonstrated the

00:56.200 --> 00:58.280
full launch countdown sequence.

00:58.280 --> 01:02.240
Key milestones as we prepare to send astronauts around the Moon.

01:02.240 --> 01:06.680
Open work at the launch pad is now underway, as technicians complete final tasks and teams

01:06.680 --> 01:11.520
prepare for a multi-day flight readiness review to assess the vehicle, ground systems, and

01:11.520 --> 01:12.760
mission readiness.

01:12.760 --> 01:17.120
Meanwhile, the Artemis II crew is preparing to enter a standard pre-launch quarantine in

01:17.120 --> 01:18.120
Houston.

01:18.120 --> 01:22.920
The roughly 14-day period helps protect astronaut health while preserving flexibility within the

01:22.920 --> 01:24.280
March launch window.

01:24.280 --> 01:28.260
America's return to the Moon is getting closer, and will continue to keep you updated every

01:28.260 --> 01:30.000
step of the way.

01:30.000 --> 01:34.260
Seven astronauts are now aboard the International Space Station, following Saturday's arrival

01:34.260 --> 01:37.780
of NASA's SpaceX Crew-12 mission.

01:37.780 --> 01:41.160
Following a successful launch, the Dragon spacecraft docked to the station's Harmony

01:41.160 --> 01:45.580
Module, officially beginning an eight-month science expedition for the crew.

01:45.580 --> 01:49.860
I think you look around the crew up here, and it's really a testament to everything

01:49.860 --> 01:50.860
that we do.

01:50.860 --> 01:54.740
We have so many countries represented, so many backgrounds, so many disciplines, and

01:54.740 --> 01:59.740
we are so excited to be here and to get to work with Expedition 74.

01:59.740 --> 02:05.300
Since arriving, the crew has quickly transitioned into life in microgravity, unloading experiments,

02:05.300 --> 02:08.900
conducting safety checks, and kicking off research that supports future missions to the

02:08.900 --> 02:12.460
Moon and Mars while delivering benefits here on Earth.

02:12.460 --> 02:16.460
And that research is getting a boost from a new tool delivered with the crew, a small

02:16.460 --> 02:20.060
but powerful lab device known as a microplate reader.

02:20.060 --> 02:24.220
About the size of a cell phone, this off-the-shelf instrument will allow astronauts to conduct

02:24.220 --> 02:27.340
biological research directly aboard the station.

02:27.340 --> 02:32.020
Its biggest advantage is speed, giving scientists real-time access to data instead of waiting

02:32.020 --> 02:35.900
for samples to be returned to Earth and analyzed in ground labs.

02:35.900 --> 02:40.420
By analyzing samples on-site, researchers can significantly shorten the path from experiment

02:40.420 --> 02:44.780
to insight, accelerating discoveries that benefit life here on Earth.

02:44.780 --> 02:49.740
NASA innovation is advancing exploration both in the atmosphere and beyond it.

02:49.740 --> 02:54.900
Engineers have developed a new low-cost camera system that reveals how air flows around rockets

02:54.900 --> 02:57.660
and aircraft inside of wind tunnels.

02:57.660 --> 03:03.100
The technique, called focused schlieren imaging, filters light similar to polarized sunglasses

03:03.100 --> 03:06.700
to capture subtle airflow changes with greater precision.

03:06.700 --> 03:10.860
Already integrated into NASA research, the technology is now available through the agency's

03:10.860 --> 03:17.260
technology transfer program, helping industry build safer, more efficient aerospace vehicles.

03:17.260 --> 03:21.140
And that same spirit of innovation is shaping deep space exploration.

03:21.140 --> 03:25.340
As NASA's carbothermal reduction demonstration recently completed prototype testing that

03:25.340 --> 03:30.420
used concentrated solar energy to extract oxygen from simulated lunar soil.

03:30.420 --> 03:35.060
By turning lunar materials and sunlight into usable resources, including potential rocket

03:35.060 --> 03:39.700
propellant, technologies like CARD could help astronauts live and work sustainably on the

03:39.700 --> 03:42.540
moon and support future missions beyond.

03:42.540 --> 03:47.100
And as we wrap up this week, Wednesday marked the five-year anniversary of NASA's Perseverance

03:47.100 --> 03:49.540
rover touching down on Mars.

03:49.540 --> 03:53.780
From that historic touchdown to today's ongoing discoveries, the mission continues to shape

03:53.780 --> 03:57.180
our path toward future exploration of the Red Planet.

03:57.180 --> 04:01.300
From the moon to Mars and beyond, the next golden age of discovery is already underway.

04:01.300 --> 04:03.580
We'll see you next time on the NASA Minute.

